rning  good morning michelle good to see you so  the fda approved this drug  without the recommendation of his  committee i mean how how  how often do they do this this is pretty  uncommon so 80 percent of the time  there's concordance between what the  advisory committee recommends and what  the fda does  in the 20 percent of time where there's  discordance three quarters of those are  instances where the advisory committee  recommends  for the drug and the fda actually says  no or puts limitations on it so this is  pretty rare  and when i spoke to dr kesleheim about  what really pushed him to resign there  were a couple things i think the first  was the big feeling  that the fda in this case did not follow  the science that there was really a lack  of efficacy for this drug  and significant concern for side effects  you know a third of the patients had  brain hemorrhage or bleeding  there was a question about why this lack  of transparency in his mind why this big  180 degree shift  from the committee being told don't  review it on the basis of surrogate  endpoints like amalek to suddenly that's  actually how they approved it  why wasn't the advisory committee called  back to assess  this idea of a circuit endpoint and then  the third issue is really  how this frustration over how are the  advisory committee's recommendations  being integrated and used  he actually pointed to a similar  approval of a muscular dystrophy drug  several years ago  where he was on the committee they voted  against it it was a surrogate endpoint  and the fda pushed it through  without really explaining why so we need  to note  tara that biogen the maker of this drug  says it submitted  to the fda millions of pages of data and  analysis from  eight clinical trials the fda said in a  statement  our review has been thorough so why are  critics like  dr kesselheim still not satisfied well  anthony there are several other issues i  think one of them  centers around this idea that the biogen 